.TH AS 1
.UC
.SH NAME
as \- assembler for the 80286
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B as
[
.B \-o
objfile
] [
.B \-b
] [
.BI \-D name\c
[\c
.BI = text\c
] ] [
.BI \-I dir
] [
.B \-ms
|
.B \-mm
|
.B \-ml
] file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I As
assembles the named file, which is 80286 and 80287 assembly in the
syntax of NASM, and writes an object file for
.IR ld (1).
It is what
.IR cc (1)
runs on the compiler's output, and what the C library's own assembly
is written for; it has as much of NASM's language as those two use,
and where an instruction may be encoded in more than one way it
writes the bytes that NASM does.
The options are:
.TP
.B \-\^o
Use
.I objfile
for the name of the object.
If this is omitted,
.I a.out
is used.
.TP
.B \-\^b
Write the bytes that the file assembles to and nothing else, as
NASM's
.B "\-f bin"
does: an image, such as a boot block.
Its addresses are those of where it is to be, which
.B org
says, and it is all text: it can have no other segment, nothing from
another file and no selector.
.TP
.BI \-\^D name
Define
.I name
as if by
.BR %define ,
to be
.I text
if that is given and nothing if not.
.TP
.BI \-\^I dir
Look in
.I dir
for a file named in an
.B %include
that is not found as it is named.
There may be four, which are tried in the order given.
.TP
.B \-\^ms
The object is for the small model, which is the default.
.TP
.B \-\^mm
The object is for the medium model.
.TP
.B \-\^ml
The object is for the large model.
.I Ld
will not link objects of different models together.
.SH LANGUAGE
A line is an optional label, an instruction or a directive with its
operands, and an optional comment, which begins with a semicolon.
A label is a name, with or without a colon after it.
A name that begins with a period is local to the last label that did
not, so that
.B .loop
may be used in each of several functions.
Names differ by the case of their letters; the names of instructions,
registers and directives do not.
.PP
The destination is the first operand.
An operand is a register, a number or an address, which are immediate,
or the contents of memory, whose address is in square brackets:
.B [bp+4]
or
.BR [es:bx+si+table] .
Where neither operand is a register the size is said with
.B byte
or
.B word
(and
.B dword
or
.B far
for a far pointer, and
.BR dword ,
.B qword
and
.B tword
for the 80287's numbers).
.B "seg name"
is the selector of the segment that
.I name
is in, which
.I ld
fills in; the name of a segment or of a group, by itself, is its
selector.
Values may be put together with
.BR "+ \- * / % << >> & ^ |" ,
and compared with
.BR "= == != <> < <= > >=" ,
which make 1 or 0, as do
.BR && ,
.B ||
and
.BR ! .
A number is decimal,
or hexadecimal with
.B 0x
before it or
.B h
after,
or a character in quotes; and numbers are reckoned with
.BR "+ \- * / %" ,
.BR "& | ^ ~" ,
.B "<< >>"
and parentheses.
.B $
is the address of the line it is on, and
.B $$
that of the start of its segment.
.PP
A far jump or call is to an address, whose segment's selector
.I ld
fills in, or to
.IB seg : offset\c
, where the segment is a number:
.BR "jmp 0:start" .
A jump is assembled short if it reaches.
A jump that does not is made long:
.B jmp
becomes the three-byte jump, and a conditional jump becomes the
opposite condition round one.
A jump said to be
.B short
is left so, and is an error if it does not reach.
.PP
The directives are:
.TP 18
.BR db ", " dw ", " dd
Bytes, words and long words: numbers, and for
.B db
strings in quotes.
A word may be an address or a
.BR seg .
.TP
.BR resb ", " resw ", " resd " n"
Room for
.I n
of them.
.TP
.BR times " n"
The instruction or directive that follows,
.I n
times.
A count that is reckoned from
.BR $ ,
as in
.BR "times 510\-($\-$$) db 0" ,
is taken to fill to a place, which stays where it is when the jumps
before it are made long.
.TP
.BR org " n"
With
.BR \-b ,
the address of the image's first byte, where it is to be: 0 if none
is given.
.TP
.BR align " n"
Advance to a multiple of
.IR n ,
with no-operation instructions, or with
.BR "align n,db b" ,
bytes
.IR b ;
in the bss with room.
.TP
.BR alignb " n"
Advance to a multiple of
.I n
with room, which is zero.
.TP
.RB name " equ " value
Define
.I name
to be the value.
.TP
.BR global " name, ..."
The names are defined here for other files to use.
.TP
.BR extern " name, ..."
The names are defined in another file.
A name may be followed by
.BR ":wrt " group ,
which is read and ignored: what it is is reckoned from where its
segment starts in the program, whatever group that is.
.TP
.BR common " name size"
.I Name
is common storage of that many bytes, which
.I ld
puts after the bss unless some file defines it.
.TP
.BR segment " name"
What follows is in that segment; also spelled
.BR section .
An object has three, the text, the data and the bss, and a segment
is one of them if it is given
.BR class=CODE ,
.B BSS
or
.B DATA
(or
.BR BEGDATA ),
or, with no class, if its name has
.B text
or
.B code
in it, or
.BR bss ,
or else is the data.
A segment of any other class, as
.BR FAR_DATA ,
is a named segment of its own (a.out(5)), which is not in the
program's data segment: up to twelve.
.TP
.BR group " name segment ..."
The segments are in the group, which
.I ld
makes one segment of the program.
The data and the bss are in the program's data segment, whatever
group they are said to be in.
.TP
.BR bits ", " cpu
These are read and ignored.
.PP
The entry point of a program is the label
.BR ..start ,
which is global without being said to be.
.PP
Before a line is assembled these are obeyed:
.TP 18
.B %include \&"file\&"
Read
.I file
here.
.TP
.BR %define " name text"
Replace
.I name
with
.I text
wherever it is found from here on, and again in what comes of that.
.TP
.BR %define " name(a,...) text"
The same, where what
.I name
is followed by in brackets takes the place of
.I a
and the others in
.IR text :
up to four.
.TP
.BR %assign " name value"
.B %define
.I name
as the value, which is reckoned here.
.TP
.B "%if value, %elif value, %else, %endif"
Assemble the lines between only if the value, which is reckoned when
the line is read, is not zero; else the lines after the first
.B %elif
whose value is not, or after the
.BR %else .
.TP
.B "%ifdef name, %ifndef name"
The same, if the name is, or is not, defined by
.B %define
or
.BR \-D .
.TP
.BR %rep " n"
The lines up to the matching
.B %endrep
are read
.I n
times.
.TP
.B %+
Between two things, makes one of them, after names are replaced:
.B "vec %+ v"
is
.B vec3
where
.I v
is 3.
.TP
.BR %macro " name n"
The lines up to
.B %endmacro
are assembled wherever
.I name
is used as an instruction, with
.B %1
to
.BI % n
replaced by its operands.
One macro may use another.
.SH FILES
.ta \w'/tmp/as*\ \ \ 'u
a.out	object
.br
/tmp/as*	temporary
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cc(1), ld(1), nm(1), a.out(5)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each is a line with the name of the file, the number of the line, and
what is wrong with it.
After any, no object is left.
.SH BUGS
Only one file is assembled.
.PP
The language is NASM's only so far as described here:
macros take no more than nine operands, and there are no local labels
in them, no structures and no listing.
.PP
NASM keeps a jump long in some programs where it would reach if
it and others were made short together;
.I as
makes them short, and its object is then a few bytes smaller than
NASM's.
.PP
An address is never written in a byte, even in an image, whose
addresses NASM knows as numbers and writes in a byte where they fit:
the two differ where an address is under 128, or in the last 128
bytes of the 64K.
